db/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097458 #edb/ref 1061 148718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097458 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "63a09a62-bd67-54f7-9a93-8dbf670b155c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097458 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097459 #edb/ref 1061 148721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097459 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "167c3d0e-c8ed-5974-9e0e-6fe077e49acc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097459 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097919 #edb/ref 1064 "Sometimes, we might have a system of equations with fractional coefficients, such as the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{4}y=-4 \\\\ 7x-5y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can turn the system into an equivalent system involving only integer coefficients. This will make it easier to solve.\n\nIn general, we multiply each equation by its lowest common denominator. In this instance, multiplying the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions $(4)$ will do the job:\n\n![](images/7f/7ff92bdf3b9c8bf5ffb571d598788f37bcd698f7b3971c337531bf9fe7f55e4e.svg)\n\nNow, we can solve the system using, for example, the elimination method. Notice that the $5y$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-5y$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable, and allows us to solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\cancel{5y} & =-16 \\\\ 7x-\\cancel{5y} & =-2 \\\\ 9x & =-18 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=-2$ into the equation $7x - 5y= -2$ to solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-5y & =-2 \\\\ 7(-2)-5y & =-2 \\\\ -14-5y & =-2 \\\\ -5y & =12 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{12}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-2, \\,y=-\\dfrac{12}{5}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097919 #edb/ref 1065 8706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097919 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "68821d79-90e2-580a-95fd-a5268f848ff4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097919 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097930 #edb/ref 1064 "We can also use the substitution method to solve systems of equations containing fractional coefficients.\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we multiply both equations by the lowest common denominator of the fractions in the system.\n\n- Then, we apply the substitution method as usual.\n\nLet's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097930 #edb/ref 1065 9138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097930 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "33f3d90d-d232-500a-b9c6-cecc2546b571" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097930 #edb/ref 1067 "Using Substitution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561562 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186562522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561562 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186562526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561562 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186562553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561562 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186562574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561562 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186562593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561562 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561562 #edb/ref 1045 "Systems of Linear Equations With Fractional Coefficients" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561562 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186562574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561562 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561562 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561562 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "193f6668-b588-534a-bb5f-0f02238fb345" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561602 #edb/ref 1080 "x=-16,\\,y=-63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561602 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561602 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0699480b-fcdd-5aa7-bd88-db69b130f0d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561706 #edb/ref 1080 "a=-4,\\,b=2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561706 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592